WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.804
Modern component frameworks and server
template engines usually make ordinary

2
00:00:04.884 --> 00:00:06.087
interpolation safer.

3
00:00:06.167 --> 00:00:10.534
A value inserted as text is escaped or
represented through structured rendering,

4
00:00:10.614 --> 00:00:13.969
and property binding avoids much manual
source construction.

5
00:00:14.049 --> 00:00:17.720
Keep autoescaping enabled and use the
framework's normal data flow.

6
00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:20.459
These defaults eliminate many routine
mistakes,

7
00:00:20.539 --> 00:00:23.577
but they do not make every application
path immune to XSS.

8
00:00:23.657 --> 00:00:27.961
Security review should concentrate on
places where developers step outside the

9
00:00:28.041 --> 00:00:28.547
default.

10
00:00:28.627 --> 00:00:32.615
Raw-HTML rendering, explicitly trusted
values, disabled autoescaping,

11
00:00:32.695 --> 00:00:36.240
helpers that mark strings as safe, direct
innerHTML assignments,

12
00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:41.067
dynamic script creation, and third-party
widgets restore interpretation boundaries.

13
00:00:41.147 --> 00:00:45.515
Each use needs a documented requirement, a
trusted source or reviewed sanitizer,

14
00:00:45.595 --> 00:00:48.000
and tests that cover later
transformations.

15
00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:52.511
One rich-content feature is not a reason
to disable escaping for an entire template

16
00:00:52.591 --> 00:00:53.477
or application.

17
00:00:53.557 --> 00:00:57.544
Server-generated state embedded in an HTML
response is another boundary.

18
00:00:57.624 --> 00:01:02.055
Plain JSON serialization followed by
arbitrary concatenation into a script block

19
00:01:02.135 --> 00:01:05.110
should not be assumed safe for every
embedding context.

20
00:01:05.190 --> 00:01:08.545
Prefer the framework's supported
state-transfer mechanism,

21
00:01:08.625 --> 00:01:13.056
a separate JSON response, or a data
container with documented safe serialization.

22
00:01:13.136 --> 00:01:15.035
Hydration does not change the rule:

23
00:01:15.115 --> 00:01:19.292
the generated document is parsed before
application code consumes its state.

24
00:01:19.372 --> 00:01:23.803
TypeScript helps express distinctions but
cannot establish them by declaration.

25
00:01:23.883 --> 00:01:26.415
Casting a string as SafeHtml,
TrustedContent,

26
00:01:26.495 --> 00:01:30.862
or another branded type does not encode,
sanitize, or verify anything at runtime.

27
00:01:30.942 --> 00:01:32.461
If a branded value is useful,

28
00:01:32.541 --> 00:01:36.909
hide its constructor and issue it only
through a runtime-controlled sanitizer or

29
00:01:36.989 --> 00:01:38.255
trusted static source.

30
00:01:38.335 --> 00:01:42.702
Review direct DOM work inside framework
components with the same source-to-sink

31
00:01:42.782 --> 00:01:44.618
model used outside the framework.

32
00:01:44.698 --> 00:01:48.939
Default protections are valuable precisely
because they narrow the risky code

33
00:01:49.019 --> 00:01:52.500
surface; escape hatches should remain
visible and exceptional.
